    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon.jpg" alt="Casimir IV Jagiellon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon">Casimir IV Jagiellon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kazimier_Jagaj%C5%82avi%C4%8D._%D0%9A%D0%B0%D0%B7%D1%96%D0%BC%D0%B5%D1%80_%D0%AF%D0%B3%D0%B0%D0%B9%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B2%D1%96%D1%87_(1645).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Pomerelia</a>, originally Polish, became a fully independent duchy in 1227 and was seized by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> in 1309, after Władysław I the Elbow-high had asked them for help against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>. Papal courts in 1320 and 1339 ordered its return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Under the Order the burden of taxation and arbitrary rule bred resistance: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno-land">Chełmno Land</a> nobility founded the Lizard League in 1397, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-grunwald">Battle of Grunwald</a> in 1410 and the heavy compensation of the 1411 Peace of Thorn, nobles and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> met in a joint assembly in 1412. In 1440 they founded the Prussian Confederation at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwidzyn">Marienwerder</a> (Kwidzyn), led by the citizens of Danzig, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elblag">Elbing</a> and Thorn.</p><p>In February 1454 the Confederation sent a delegation under Jan Bażyński to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon">Casimir IV Jagiellon</a>, declaring him the only true sovereign of their lands. On 6 March 1454 the Royal Chancellery issued the Incorporation Act, which accepted the Prussians as subjects, incorporated Prussia into the Polish kingdom and granted considerable autonomy. The war that followed ended in October 1466 with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-peace-of-thorn">Second Peace of Thorn</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The province took in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Pomerelia</a> with the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> and the city of Danzig, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno">Chełmno</a> and Michałów Lands with Thorn, the Prince-Bishopric of Warmia with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frombork">Frauenburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lidzbark-warminski">Heilsberg</a> and Allenstein, and the Marienburger Land with Marienburg and the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elblag">Elbing</a>. At the start of the 15th century the Order's lands held a mixed population: about 200,000 Germans, 140,000 native Prussians and about 140,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>; Poles were two thirds of Pomerelia's people and half of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno-land">Chełmno Land</a>'s. About 110,000 people, nearly a quarter of the population, lived in 93 towns.</p><p>Trade was central to the province's privileges. The Prussian estates were exempted from the Pfundzoll and guaranteed freedom of trade, and Thorn and Danzig kept the right to mint coins. In 1529 a monetary union joined Royal Prussia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-prussia">Ducal Prussia</a> and the rest of the kingdom. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno-law">Kulm law</a> guaranteed inheritance also in the female line, which led to the fragmentation of estates; in 1522 the nobility demanded the Polish model of inheritance and land ownership.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/second-peace-of-thorn"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-peace-of-thorn.jpg" alt="Second Peace of Thorn" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/second-peace-of-thorn">Second Peace of Thorn</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2nd_peace_of_torun.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Marian Jaroczyński (from 1873), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Royal Prussia was born of war. After the Prussian Confederation pledged allegiance to Casimir IV on 6 March 1454, the Thirteen Years' War, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirteen-years-war-1454-1466">War of the Cities</a>, began; the king appointed Hans von Baysen (Jan Bażyński) war-time governor and took oaths from the citizens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torun">Toruń</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elblag">Elbląg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a>. The war also drew in cities of the Order's eastern lands such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kneiphof">Kneiphof</a>. The Knights won the Battle of Chojnice in 1454 but could not pay for enough knights to retake the castles held by the insurgents, and thirteen years of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attrition">attrition</a> ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-peace-of-thorn">Second Peace of Thorn</a> in October 1466.</p><p>Later conflicts were smaller. A short war, the so-called priests' war, settled the claim of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Warmia to independence in the king's favour in 1479. In 1498 Frederick of Saxony, elected Grand Master of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>, pursued a hostile policy to reclaim lost territories. In the Order's last war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, in 1519–1521, Albrecht von Hohenzollern was defeated, and the Polish General Assembly met at Toruń in 1519 and at Bydgoszcz in 1520.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/malbork"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malbork.jpg" alt="Malbork" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/malbork">Malbork</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Castillo_de_Malbork,_Polonia,_2013-05-19,_DD_05.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Royal Prussia kept its existing law codes and borders, and only Prussians could be appointed to its offices (ius indigenatus). It was divided into voivodeships on the Polish model: after 1466 Pomeranian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno">Chełmno</a> and Marienburg (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malbork">Malbork</a>). By a decision of the Polish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> in 1467 its main governing body was the Prussian council (Landesrat): three voivodes, three castellans, three chamberlains and two delegates from each of Thorn, Danzig and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elblag">Elbing</a>, later joined by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Warmia (1479) and Chełmno (1482), 17 members in all. From 1508 the bishop of Warmia headed it. Because the council could not tax without the commons, an assembly of the estates grew into a bicameral Prussian parliament in 1512–1526.</p><p>The kings governed through a governor, an office abolished in 1467, then a starosta general from 1472, which Stibor von Baysen refused in protest. Nicolaus von Baysen led the opposition from 1485 against violations of the ius indigenatus. The Statute of Prussia of 1506, a central treasury and a supreme tribunal of 1511 brought the province closer to the kingdom; Danzig decided its cases internally.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lucas-watzenrode"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lucas-watzenrode.jpg" alt="Lucas Watzenrode" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lucas-watzenrode">Lucas Watzenrode</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C5%81ukasz_Watzenrode.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The province was the meeting ground of Polish and Prussian political cultures. Its estates stood for deep particularism, sent only token delegations to the royal elections of 1492, 1501 and 1506, and yet were drawn step by step into the kingdom: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucas-watzenrode">Lucas Watzenrode</a> took part in a Senate meeting in 1509 as the first representative of the Prussian council, summons to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> went to the Prussian sejmiks from 1537, and in 1548 both Prussian chambers sent deputies and senators to the Sejm. Closer ties found support above all among the ordinary nobility, who found Polish political and legal solutions more favourable.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> also crossed into Royal Prussia. After the Order's state was secularised as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia</a> under the Lutheran Albrecht, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> spread especially in the big cities, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a> was declared the ruling religion in Danzig, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elblag">Elbing</a> and Thorn after 1526, though part of their population remained Catholic. Albrecht had Luther's Catechism translated into Old Prussian, Polish and Lithuanian to unite the people of both Prussias, while the Polish king tried to stop the spread of Protestantism.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/union-of-lublin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/union-of-lublin.jpg" alt="Union of Lublin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/union-of-lublin">Union of Lublin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Unia_Lubelska_1569_r..jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Royal Prussia's autonomy ended in 1569 with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-lublin">Union of Lublin</a>, which created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>. The province was integrated fully into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a>, its parliament was reduced to a provincial assembly and incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> of the Commonwealth, and its other separate institutions were dissolved. Before the union was concluded, the Polish chancellor openly admitted that the act breached the law by violating the privileges granted in 1454.</p><p>Its lands were thereafter governed as the Pomeranian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno">Chełmno</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malbork">Malbork</a> Voivodeships and the Prince-Bishopric of Warmia. Across the border, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia</a> passed in 1618 into personal union with the Electorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> and in 1657, by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-wehlau">Treaty of Wehlau</a>, gained full independence, allowing its later elevation to a kingdom. In the first and second partitions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, in 1772 and 1793, the Kingdom of Prussia, the successor state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>, annexed the former territory of Royal Prussia and reorganised it as the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-prussia">West Prussia</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Royal Prussia was the western half of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>'s Prussia, which its nobles and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> took into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a> in 1454 and won in the Thirteen Years' War. Self-governing under its own council and parliament until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-lublin">Union of Lublin</a> of 1569, it was annexed by the Kingdom of Prussia in the partitions of 1772 and 1793.</em></p>

